Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: 
“Company Material Adverse Effect” means any event, change, occurrence or effect that, either individually or when aggregated with any other, (a) has had, or would reasonably be expected to have, a material adverse impact on the overall business, financial condition, or results of operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries, or (b) would, or would reasonably be expected to, prevent, significantly delay, or materially impair the ability of the Company to consummate the transaction contemplated by this Agreement. 

Notwithstanding the generality of the foregoing, in connection with clause (b) only, no such event, change, occurrence or effect shall constitute a Company Material Adverse Effect if it arises solely as a consequence of any of the following factors: 
   (i) fluctuations in general economic conditions or standard market cycles that similarly affect other companies operating in the same industry; 
   (ii) broad-based changes in globally recognized accounting principles or regulatory guidelines which uniformly apply across the sector; 
   (iii) natural disaster events or other force majeure incidents, provided that their impact is not materially disproportionate compared to industry peers; 
   (iv) variations in interest or exchange rates resulting in market volatility. 

(Pages 3-5 of the Agreement)
